WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.760
Why would a young neuron make the first protein of an immune cascade?

2
00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:12.300
Complement is a set of proteins that coat microbes and dead cells.

3
00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:15.720
They coat them so that eating cells will swallow them.

4
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:21.960
Yet in the young mouse, retinal neurons make C1q, the first complement protein.

5
00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:26.320
It turned out to be a clue to how the brain removes connections it built too many

6
00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:27.320
of.

7
00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:29.890
The best studied case is the visual thalamus.

8
00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:34.210
In a newborn mouse, inputs from the two eyes overlap there.

9
00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:38.290
Over roughly the first week and a half, they sort into separate territories.

10
00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:40.830
Inputs that lose are removed.

11
00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:43.610
Which inputs go, and what takes them away?

12
00:00:44.433 --> 00:00:46.333
The first part of the answer is a tag.

13
00:00:47.453 --> 00:00:49.093
C1q binds a target.

14
00:00:49.673 --> 00:00:53.213
A chain of cuts follows, and C3 is cut in two.

15
00:00:53.973 --> 00:01:00.713
The big piece, C3b, can fix to a nearby surface, then is trimmed to iC3b.

16
00:01:01.473 --> 00:01:07.113
In the young visual thalamus, C1q and C3 lay at developing synapses.

17
00:01:08.117 --> 00:01:09.717
The second part is the eater.

18
00:01:10.377 --> 00:01:14.217
Microglia are the brain's resident immune cells and its main phagocytes.

19
00:01:14.877 --> 00:01:20.897
A phagocyte with complement receptor 3 binds iC3b, which promotes engulfment of what

20
00:01:20.897 --> 00:01:25.837
it coats. In the mouse thalamus, researchers found retinal material inside

21
00:01:25.837 --> 00:01:29.477
microglial lysosomes, the digestive compartments of the cell.

22
00:01:29.997 --> 00:01:33.137
A dye that glows only in acid gave the same answer.

23
00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:35.110
Does the eater choose?

24
00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:39.730
Silence one eye, and microglia held more material from that eye.

25
00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:43.870
Boost one eye instead, and they held more from the untreated eye.

26
00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:49.190
This compared whole eyes, and it does not show how activity becomes a tag.

27
00:01:49.970 --> 00:01:55.030
Still, in this circuit, microglia preferentially remove less active inputs.

28
00:01:55.833 --> 00:01:57.873
Now take away the reader of the tag.

29
00:01:58.453 --> 00:02:04.293
Without complement receptor 3, or without C3, microglia took up roughly half as

30
00:02:04.293 --> 00:02:09.573
much. Half of the uptake remained without the receptor, so other routes contribute.

31
00:02:10.193 --> 00:02:15.193
A month after birth, those mice kept more retinal synapses and more overlap between

32
00:02:15.193 --> 00:02:20.993
the eyes. Keep this rule: in the mouse visual thalamus, complement tags, microglia

33
00:02:20.993 --> 00:02:24.013
eat, and less active inputs are eaten more.
